WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.580
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.540
This episode has been sponsored by Lambda
Labs.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:13.650
Not so long ago, we talked about DeepMind’s
AlphaStar, an AI that was able to defeat top-tier

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.630
human players in Starcraft 2, a complex real-time
strategy game.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:23.869
Of course, I love talking about AI’s that
are developed to challenge pro gamers at a

00:00:23.869 --> 00:00:30.130
variety of difficult games, so this time around,
we’ll have a look another major milestone,

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:37.040
OpenAI Five, which is an AI that plays DOTA2,
a multiplayer online battle arena game with

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.880
a huge cult following.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.750
As this game requires long-term strategic
planning, it is a classic nightmare scenario

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.390
for any AI.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:52.860
But OpenAI is no stranger to DOTA2, in 2017
they showed us an initial version of their

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:59.789
AI that was able to play 1 versus 1 games
with only one hero and was able to reliably

00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:02.899
beat Dendi, a world champion player.

00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:08.200
That was quite an achievement, however, of
course, this was meant to be a stepping stone

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.619
towards playing the real DOTA2.

00:01:10.619 --> 00:01:17.979
Then, in 2018, they unveiled OpenAI Five,
an improved version of this AI that played

00:01:17.979 --> 00:01:20.869
5 versus 5 games with a limited hero pool.

00:01:20.869 --> 00:01:26.340
This team was able to defeat competent players,
but was still not quite at the level of a

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.359
world champion human team.

00:01:28.359 --> 00:01:33.329
In a one-hour interview, the OpenAI research
team mentioned that due to the deadline of

00:01:33.329 --> 00:01:37.099
The International event, they had to make
quite a few concessions.

00:01:37.099 --> 00:01:40.200
And this time, several things have changed.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:47.079
First, they didn’t just challenge some local
team of formidable players, no-no, they flat

00:01:47.079 --> 00:01:50.959
out challenged OG, the reigning world champion
team.

00:01:50.959 --> 00:01:55.079
An ambitious move, that exudes confidence
from their side.

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:59.859
Second, this time around, there was no tight
deadline as the date of the challenge was

00:01:59.859 --> 00:02:01.450
chosen by OpenAI.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:07.950
Let’s quickly talk about the rules of the
competition and then …see if OpenAI’s

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.209
confident move was justified!

00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:14.489
These learning agents don’t look at the
pixels of the game, and as a result, they

00:02:14.489 --> 00:02:16.709
see the world as a big bunch of numbers.

00:02:16.709 --> 00:02:23.500
And this time around, it was able to play
a pool of 17 heroes, and trained against itself

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.319
for millions and millions of games.

00:02:26.319 --> 00:02:30.900
And now, let’s have a look at what happened
in this best of 3 series!

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:36.530
In match 1, right after picking the roster
of heroes, the AI estimated its win probability

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:42.850
to be 67%, so it was quite a surprise that
early on it looked like OpenAI’s bots were

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:45.090
running around aimlessly.

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:50.370
Over time, we found out that it was not at
all the case - it plays unusually aggressively

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:56.640
from the get-go and uses buybacks quite liberally
at times when human players don’t really

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.000
consider it to be a good choice.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:04.030
These buybacks resurrect a perished hero quickly
but in return, cost money.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:09.730
Later, it became clearer that these bots are
no joke: they know exactly when to engage

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:13.879
and when to back out from an engagement with
the smallest sliver of health left.

00:03:13.879 --> 00:03:18.840
I will show quite a few examples of those
to you during this video, so stay tuned.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:25.700
A little less than 20 minutes in, we had a
very even game 1, if anything, OpenAI seemed

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:31.750
a tiny bit behind, and someone noted that
we should perhaps ask the bots what they think

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:33.319
about their chances.

00:03:33.319 --> 00:03:40.180
And then the AI said, yeah, no worries, we
have a higher than 95% chance to win the game.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:44.200
This was such a pivotal moment that was very
surprising for everyone.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:48.700
Of course, if you you call out a win with
confidence, you better go all the way and

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.550
indeed win the game.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:51.569
Right?

00:03:51.569 --> 00:03:52.590
Right.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:58.459
And sure enough, they wiped out almost the
entire world champion team of the human players

00:03:58.459 --> 00:04:15.159
immediately after.

00:04:15.159 --> 00:04:18.620
And then noted, you know what, remember that
we just said?

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:19.670
Forget that.

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:24.050
We estimate our chances to win to be above
99% now.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:27.430
And shortly after, they won match number one.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:28.690
Can you believe this?

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.930
This is absolutely amazing.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:36.590
Interestingly, one of the developers said
that the AI is great at assessing whether

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:38.000
a fight is worth it.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.630
As an interesting corollary, if you engage
with it and it fights you, it probably means

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:45.130
you are going to lose.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:47.710
That must be quite confusing for the players.

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:49.099
Some mind games for you.

00:04:49.099 --> 00:04:50.240
Love it.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:55.789
At the event, it was also such a joy to see
such a receptive audience that understood

00:04:55.789 --> 00:04:59.330
and appreciated high-level plays.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:01.560
Onwards to match number two.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:06.090
Right after the draft, which is the process
of choosing the heroes for each team, the

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:11.410
AI predicted a win percentage that was much
closer this time around, around 60%.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:18.120
In this game, the AI turned up the heat real
fast, and said just 5 minutes into the game,

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:23.760
which is nothing, that it has an over 80%
chance to win this game.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:25.820
And now, watch this.

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:30.660
Early in this game, you can see a great example
of where the AI just gets away with a sliver

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:31.830
of health.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:57.090
Look at this guy.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.090
Look at that!

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:03.450
This is either an accident or some unreal-level
foresight from the side of this agent.

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:06.699
I’d love to hear your opinion on which one
you think it is.

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:13.300
By the 9 and a half minute mark, which is
still really early, OpenAI Five said yes,

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:15.319
we got this one too.

00:06:15.319 --> 00:06:18.270
Over 95%.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:23.639
Here you see in interesting scenario where
the AI loses one hero, but it almost immediately

00:06:23.639 --> 00:06:29.440
kills two of the human heroes, and comes out
favorably, at which point we wonder whether

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:36.330
this was a deliberate bait it pulled on the
humans.

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:57.080
By the 15-minute mark, the human players lost
a barracks and were heavily underfunded and

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:00.580
outplayed with seemingly no way to come back.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:04.620
And sure enough, by the 21-minute mark, the
game was over.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:10.009
There is no other way to say it, this second
game was a one-sided beatdown.

00:07:10.009 --> 00:07:14.940
Game 1 was a strategic back and forth where
OpenAI Five waited for the right moment to

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:20.610
win the game in a big team fight, where here,
they pressured human team from the get-go

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:25.250
and never let them reach the endgame where
they might have and advantage with their picks.

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:35.419
Also, have a look at this.

00:07:35.419 --> 00:07:44.189
Unreal.

00:07:44.189 --> 00:07:47.870
The final result is 2 to 0 for OpenAI.

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:53.099
In the post match interview, N0tail, one of
the human players noted that he is confident

00:07:53.099 --> 00:07:59.220
that from 5 games, they would take at least
one, and after 15 games, they would start

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:01.020
winning reliably.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:05.400
Very reminiscent of what we have heard from
players playing against DeepMind’s AI in

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:08.690
StarCraft 2 and I hope this will be tested.

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:14.240
However, in the end, he agreed that it is
inevitable that this AI will become unbeatable

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:15.370
at some point.

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:21.449
It was also noted that in 5v5 fights, they
seem better in planning than any human team

00:08:21.449 --> 00:08:25.599
is and there is quite a lot to learn from
the AI for us humans.

00:08:25.599 --> 00:08:30.000
They were also trying to guess the reasoning
for all of these early buybacks.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.810
According to the players, initially, they
flat out seemed like misplays.

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:39.171
Perhaps the reason for these instant and not
really great buybacks might have been the

00:08:39.171 --> 00:08:44.720
fact that the AI knows that if the game goes
on for much longer, statistically, their chances

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:49.730
with their given composition to win the game
dwindles, so it needs to immediately go and

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:52.940
win right now, whatever the cost.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:58.030
And again, an important lesson is that in
this project, OpenAI is not spending so much

00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:01.250
money and resources to just play video games.

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:08.190
DOTA2 is a wonderful testbed to see how their
AI compares to humans at complex tasks that

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:10.400
involve strategy and teamwork.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:15.950
However, the ultimate goal is to reuse parts
of this system for other complex problems

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:18.130
outside of video games.

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:23.140
For example, the algorithm that you’ve seen
here today can also do this.

00:09:23.140 --> 00:09:25.880
But wait, there’s more.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:30.770
Players after these showmatches always tend
to get these messages from others on Twitter

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:34.340
telling them what they did wrong and what
they should have done instead.

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:40.600
Well, luckily, these people were able to show
their prowess as OpenAI gave the chance for

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:46.900
anyone in the world to challenge the OpenAI
Five competitively and play against them online.

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:51.970
This way, not only team OG, but everyone can
get crushed by the AI.

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:53.250
How cool is that?

00:09:53.250 --> 00:10:00.410
This Arena event has concluded with over 15000
games played where OpenAI Five had a 99.4%

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:01.410
winrate.

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:07.010
There are still ways to beat it, but given
the rate of progress of this project, likely

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:08.730
not for long.

00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:09.820
Insanity.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:14.760
As always, if you are interested in more details,
I put a link to a reddit AMA in the video

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:20.060
description, and I also can’t wait to pick
the algorithm apart for you, but for now,

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:22.780
we’ll have to wait for the full paper to
appear.

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:26.810
And note that what happened here is not to
be underestimated.

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:32.260
Huge respect to the OpenAI team, to OG for
the amazing games and congratulations to the

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:36.320
humans who were able to beat these beastly
bots online.

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:41.620
So there you go, another long video that’s
not two minutes, and it’s not about a paper.

00:10:41.620 --> 00:10:42.620
Yet.

